  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1243 AM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nCalm and mild conditions will continue on Thursday, though \ntemperatures will drop a few degrees thanks to a dry cold front \npassing through.\n\nStarting on Friday, our next incoming source of moisture will be a \ndeepening trough over the Pacific northwest. As we move into this \nweekend, southwest flow aloft will increase over the region, \nbringing in moist Pacific air and better forcing to western \nColorado. Meanwhile, deterministic guidance suggests a shortwave \npassing south of our area during the day on Friday, which could \npotentially bring some rain to the far southeast plains as well. \nHowever, if the wave picks up in speed, most of the precipitation \ncould miss us, falling in Kansas instead.\n\nEither way, the deepening trough out west will likely bring \naccumulating snow to the mountains this weekend. Showers will start \nmore isolated in nature, increasing in coverage later in the weekend \nand into early next week. Latest guidance shows the upper trough \namplifying, with a secondary low potentially moving onshore from the \nsouthwest as the trough digs into the Four Corners. Details on the \ntiming, strength, and path of the system still need to be refined in \nthe data, but overall cooler and more active weather is currently \nexpected for next week.",
